SoX Resampler Library       Copyright (c) 2007-18 robs@users.sourceforge.net

The SoX Resampler library `libsoxr' performs one-dimensional sample-rate
conversion -- it may be used, for example, to resample PCM-encoded audio.
For higher-dimensional resampling, such as for visual-image processing, you
should look elsewhere.

It aims to give fast¹ and very high quality² results for any constant
(rational or irrational) resampling ratio.  Phase-response, preserved
bandwidth, aliasing, and rejection level parameters are all configurable;
alternatively, simple `preset' configurations may be selected.  A
variable-rate resampling mode of operation is also included.

The resampler is currently available either as part of `libsox' (the audio
file-format and effect library), or stand-alone as `libsoxr' (this package).
The interfaces to libsox and libsoxr are slightly different, with that of
libsoxr designed specifically for resampling.  An application requiring
support for other effects, or for reading-from or writing-to audio files or
devices, should use libsox (or other libraries such as libsndfile or
libavformat).

Libsoxr provides a simple API that allows interfacing using the most
commonly-used sample formats and buffering schemes: sample-formats may be
either floating-point or integer, and multiple channels either interleaved
or split in separate buffers.  The API is documented in the header file
`soxr.h', together with sample code found in the 'examples' directory.

For compatibility with the popular `libsamplerate' library, the header file
`soxr-lsr.h' is provided and may be used as an alternative API.³  Note
however, that libsoxr does not provide a full emulation of libsamplerate
and that using this approach, only a sub-set of libsoxr's features are
available.

The design was inspired by Laurent De Soras' paper `The Quest For The
Perfect Resampler', http://ldesoras.free.fr/doc/articles/resampler-en.pdf;
in essence, it combines Julius O. Smith's `Bandlimited Interpolation'
technique (https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/resample/resample.pdf) with FFT-
based over-sampling.

Note that for real-time resampling, libsoxr may have a higher latency
than non-FFT based resamplers.  For example, when using the `High Quality'
configuration to resample between 44100Hz and 48000Hz, the latency is
around 1000 output samples, i.e. roughly 20ms (though passband and FFT-
size configuration parameters may be used to reduce this figure).

Streaming peak observation
---------------------------

In addition to the delivered PCM, a fixed-ratio resampler can optionally
report the largest normalised magnitude seen over a sliding window of the
most recent output, together with its exact position.  This is a true
interpolated peak (not a count of integer clips, and not a scan of the
caller's already-quantised samples): each integer output frame is examined
at four phases -- the integer sample and three quarter-frame interpolants.
The interpolant at k + p/4 (p = 1, 2, 3) is a Hann-windowed sinc of 32 taps
(j = -15..16),

    w(j, p) = sinc(p/4 - j) * (1 + cos(pi*(p/4 - j)/16)) / 2,

with the 32 weights normalised to sum to one; samples before the start and
after the end of the stream are taken as zero.

Enable it, after soxr_create() (or after soxr_clear()) and before any input
is processed, with

    soxr_set_peak_window(resampler, W);     /* W in 1..8192 output frames */

The window may only be configured while idle; out-of-range windows,
variable-rate (SOXR_VR) resamplers, and reconfiguration mid-stream return an
error and leave the existing state untouched.  soxr_clear() drops the
history and counters but keeps the window configuration.

Query with soxr_peak_query() (one channel, or all channels merged by
passing num_channels) or soxr_peak_query_all(); the returned soxr_peak_info_t
gives the number of frames delivered, the number of frame units whose four
phases have completed observation, the current window start, and the peak's
magnitude, channel, integer frame and quarter-frame phase (0..3).  A frame
unit completes only once its 16 future frames exist, so observation lags the
output by 16 frames; the last 16 units complete when the stream is flushed.
Ordinary input starvation or a zero-capacity output call is not an end of
stream, and repeated draining or querying never advances the state.  An
empty window is reported with valid == 0.

The measured point is the normalised output after io_spec.scale has been
applied but before rounding, saturation and dithering, so for integer
output types the magnitude can exceed 1 (the saturation itself is still
reported by soxr_num_clips).  Observation works through the split fast
path, interleaved output and the soxr_output() input callback, and adds no
work to an instance that never enables it.

